{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The problem asks for the single mechanism best explaining why the nighttime UHI gap is larger in some cities than in others. SRC_001 asserts that this mechanism is the absence of evapotranspiration/impervious cover. But this mechanism primarily explains why the city is warmer in absolute terms, not why the gap varies across cities. The claim that 'cities with greater impervious cover... produce a larger nocturnal temperature gap' is contradicted by the target's own countercondition: arid cities sit over naturally low-ET countryside, so the city–rural ET differential is small, yet arid cities often show the largest nocturnal UHI — driven by the rural surface's rapid radiative cooling (high sky view factor), not by the city's loss of latent cooling. By its own stated falsifier, arid cities with large UHI undermine the universality of the ET-deficit explanation, yet the target still presents ET deficit as the single dominant cross-city modulator.","premise":"The target commits to a cross-city modulator and acknowledges that if arid cities with naturally low rural evapotranspiration still show large nocturnal UHI, the mechanism's universality is challenged. Arid regions are precisely where rural ET is low, so the city–rural ET gap cannot account for their large nocturnal UHI — indicating a different mechanism (e.g., differential radiative cooling / sky view factor) dominates the cross-city variation.","premise_evidence":[{"block":"8d1e9b2a3c4f5d6e7f8a9b0c","quote":"The single dominant cross-city modulator is the absence of evapotranspiration: cities with more impervious surface cover and less green space lose the latent heat cooling pathway, so nocturnal cooling rates are slower and the nighttime UHI gap is larger."},{"block":"2a3b4c5d6e7f8a9b0c1d2e3f","quote":"If arid cities (naturally low rural evapotranspiration) still show large nocturnal UHI, the mechanism's universality is challenged"}],"counterexample":["An arid city such as Phoenix has large impervious cover but sits over desert with negligible rural evapotranspiration, so the city–countryside ET deficit is minimal; yet Phoenix exhibits a strong nocturnal UHI, better explained by rapid radiative cooling of the open desert (high sky view factor) versus slow cooling of urban canyons."],"target_alias":"SRC_001"}]}